#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344236 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa3dc44f-86e9-58c8-96da-3f517ff52007" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Triple Integral as a Mixed or Repeated Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2281b5d8-2a29-52ff-ac92-7b3ac7d5b0e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}y+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344238} {:answer/id #uuid "ad8ce24f-1c15-5c95-bc11-61cc63059ff6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-x-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344239} {:answer/id #uuid "09a0fb5a-e2f9-5dcb-9aed-ee906167c0a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344240} {:answer/id #uuid "bc8c718c-8048-5446-a15f-91f36a7ba1f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344241} {:answer/id #uuid "fc79813d-2bfd-5742-b427-85fd7cbf7d0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6-x-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344242} {:answer/id #uuid "32e657ea-6ba6-53f5-a5c8-44d39de51533" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32e657ea-6ba6-53f5-a5c8-44d39de51533" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344279} :answer-field/id #uuid "8212b04b-1b2e-51a7-b8e6-54f518b38a70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "05d837ac-69f8-5c45-8fb6-a9ac4817dd42" :question/math-academy-id "q-161496" :question/problem "The triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{∗}^{∗}{\\int }_{∗}^{∗}f(x,y,z)\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\nis defined over the type I region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,\\frac{1}{2}y\\le x\\le 2,\\,1\\le y\\le 4,\\,0\\le z\\le 4-x-y}.\n$$\n\nUse $[a,b]$ as the full nonempty projection of the exactly specified region $R$ onto the $y$ axis. The inner limits may be piecewise. What is $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "The inequalities defining $R$ are retained exactly. A fiber is nonempty only when $x\\le4-y$. Combining this with $x\\ge y/2$ gives $y/2\\le4-y$, hence $y\\le8/3$. Every $y\\in[1,8/3]$ is attained, for example at $x=y/2$, $z=0$. Therefore the full nonempty projection is $[a,b]=[1,8/3]$ and\n\n$$a+b=1+\\frac83=\\frac{11}{3}.$$\n\nThe actual base is $D=\\{(x,y):1\\le y\\le8/3,\\ y/2\\le x\\le\\min(2,4-y)\\}$. The upper $x$ bounds meet at $y=2$, so the triple integral is\n\n$$\\iiint_R f\\,dV=\\int_1^2\\int_{y/2}^2\\int_0^{4-x-y}f(x,y,z)\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy+\\int_2^{8/3}\\int_{y/2}^{4-y}\\int_0^{4-x-y}f(x,y,z)\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy.$$\n\nFor $f=1$, these terms are $35/24$ and $1/9$, giving volume $113/72$. Using the printed nominal limits $1\\le y\\le4$ and $y/2\\le x\\le2$ instead includes reversed $z$ bounds. That formal signed integral gives $9/8$ and does not integrate over $R$. The source accepted the printed-endpoint sum $5$; the corrected study question asks for the full actual projection and adds $11/3$ as the correct choice."}]}
